{"schema_version":1,"event_id":"bob-cli-78:000001:issue_created:bob-cli-78:817a2c5862f644d6a51ffd3a53ac02d76663ae385022e6cc5572e38b0f37e7a6","timestamp":"2026-10-13T07:14:27Z","actor":"bbugyi200.apollo.71","operation":"issue_created","issue_id":"bob-cli-78","payload":{"kind":"issue_created","issue":{"id":"bob-cli-78","title":"Sub-project cascade for project blocking and the project pull-in","status":"open","issue_type":"plan","tier":"epic","parent_id":null,"owner":"bryanbugyi34@gmail.com","assignee":"","created_at":"2026-10-13T07:14:27Z","created_by":"bbugyi200.apollo.71","updated_at":"2026-10-13T07:14:27Z","closed_at":null,"close_reason":null,"description":"A blocked project's sub-projects (project notes whose `parent:` names it, recursively) are Blocked too: their `^prj` and every open resident are `[?]` and tagged `#prj_is_blocked`. `bob task reconcile` derives this and reverses it when the ancestor's reasons clear. The Task Card (Ctrl+Shift+P) applies and reverses it in the same gesture. Every other engine reads a tagged `^prj` as an inherited reason, so nothing links, recovers, or pulls in a cascade-blocked task. Closing a sub-project's `^prj` that leaves its parent with no open work pulls the parent's `^prj` in as Next (§12.10 trigger (c)), both in bob capture and in Obsidian.","design":"plan:202610/subproject_cascade.md","model":"","is_ready_to_work":false,"changespec_name":"","changespec_bug_id":"","creation_reason":"Approved epic plan plan:202610/subproject_cascade.md requests this work","dependencies":[]}}}
